---
status: 'published';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Guides - The Right-Sized Website"
  description="A practical guide to choosing the right website structure for your goals, audience, and work."
>
  <!-- Hero with background image -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
    <div class="absolute inset-0">
      <img
        src="/images/guides/night-sky-constellation.webp"
        alt=""
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-40 dark:opacity-50"
      />
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-white/80 via-white/50 to-white/10 dark:from-gray-950/85 dark:via-gray-950/70 dark:to-gray-950/40"
      >
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="relative z-10">
      <div
        class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-24 md:py-32 text-center"
      >
        <p
          class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 mb-2"
        >
          Guides
        </p>
        <h1
          class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-serif font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
        >
          The Right-Sized Website
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A website should fit the work. Some projects need a single clear page.
          Others need depth, story, resources, booking, publishing, or something
          more custom. The goal is to choose the right amount of structure for
          where you are now.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Content -->
  <section class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 md:py-24">
      <!-- Section 1 - Start here -->
      <section id="start-here" class="mb-16 md:mb-24">
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 md:gap-10 items-start">
          <div>
            <h2
              class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
            >
              Start with the role of the site.
            </h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
              Many people start by asking which platform they should use. A
              better first question is what role the website needs to play.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
              For some businesses, profiles, referrals, and a simple online
              presence answer most questions. For others, the website needs to
              build trust, showcase work, explain an approach, support a
              community, or guide people through a more involved decision.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              The right website has enough structure to support your goals
              without adding complexity that does not serve the visitor.
            </p>
          </div>

          <figure
            class="relative rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
          >
            <img
              src="/images/guides/circle-pattern-black-white.webp"
              alt="Abstract circle pattern diagram suggesting structure and relationships"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            />
            <figcaption class="sr-only">
              Visual: abstract structure map
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2 - Choosing the right structure -->
      <section id="structure" class="mb-16 md:mb-24">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
        >
          Choosing the right structure
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
          Most website decisions become easier when you answer three questions:
          what the site should accomplish, how often it will change, and who
          will maintain it.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Outcome
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Are you trying to generate inquiries, build trust, show a
              portfolio, publish ideas, support a community, or facilitate
              transactions?
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Rhythm
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Will the site change rarely, occasionally, regularly, or often? A
              site that rarely changes can be built differently from one that
              depends on ongoing publishing.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Ownership
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Do you want to edit the site yourself, get help when needed, or
              set it up carefully and leave it mostly alone?
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 - The art of a website -->
      <section
        id="art"
        class="mb-16 md:mb-24 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6 md:p-10"
      >
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
        >
          The art of a website
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
          Structure makes a site understandable. Expression makes it memorable.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          Typography, spacing, imagery, color, and language all communicate
          something before a visitor reads every word. A good website does more
          than organize information. It creates an impression of the people,
          ideas, and work behind it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 - Common website patterns -->
      <section id="patterns" class="mb-16 md:mb-24">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
        >
          Common website patterns
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
          Different kinds of work need different kinds of clarity. These
          patterns are starting points, not fixed rules.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Consultant or coach
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Visitors need to understand what you do, who it helps, and what
              happens next.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Often needs: Home, Services, About, Proof, Contact or Book.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Boutique studio or creative practice
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Visitors need confidence in your taste, process, perspective, and
              fit.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Often needs: Home, Work, Case Studies, Approach, Contact.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Local service
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Visitors need practical answers quickly: what you do, where you
              work, why they should trust you, and how to reach you.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Often needs: one strong page or a light multi-page site.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Restaurant or cafe
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Visitors need hours, location, menu, reservations, ordering, and a
              quick sense of the place.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Often needs: Home, Menu, Hours, Location, Contact or Reserve.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Nonprofit or community organization
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Visitors need to understand the mission, find useful information,
              and see how they can participate or contribute.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Often needs: Home, About, Programs, Events, Resources, Support.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2"
            >
              Creator, writer, or educator
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Visitors need context, useful material, and a reason to return.
              The content itself becomes part of the experience.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Often needs: Home, Articles, Resources, About, Subscribe.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 - Common building blocks -->
      <section id="capabilities" class="mb-16 md:mb-24">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
        >
          Common building blocks
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
          Think in capabilities before choosing tools. Most websites are
          assembled from a small set of useful parts. You do not need all of
          them.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 max-w-4xl">
          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Booking
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              A simple way to schedule calls, sessions, visits, or appointments.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Forms
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Contact, inquiry, intake, applications, or feedback routed
              clearly.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Proof
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Testimonials, case studies, galleries, examples, logos, or
              results.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Publishing
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Articles, resources, updates, newsletters, or educational
              material.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Transactions
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Checkout links, invoices, deposits, donations, or other payment
              paths.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Search presence
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Clear page titles, accurate profiles, readable structure, and
              basic discoverability.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Analytics
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Simple signals to learn what people visit, read, click, or ignore.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-5"
          >
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
              Performance and accessibility
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              A site that loads quickly, reads clearly, and works for more
              people by default.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 - Modern tools -->
      <section id="modern-tools" class="mb-16 md:mb-24">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
        >
          Modern tools can help, but judgment still matters.
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
          AI and lightweight prototypes can make it easier to explore structure,
          draft copy, compare options, and test the feel of a site before
          committing to a direction.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl">
          Used well, these tools reduce rework. They do not replace taste, care,
          or an understanding of the people the site is meant to serve.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7 - How I think about website projects -->
      <section id="process" class="mb-16 md:mb-24">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
        >
          How I think about website projects
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
          I usually start by understanding the work, the audience, and the
          resources available to maintain the site. From there, we can decide
          what belongs now, what can wait, and what kind of structure makes
          sense.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-4 gap-6">
          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1.5"
            >
              Clarify
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Define the purpose, audience, and desired outcome.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1.5"
            >
              Structure
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Choose the pages, pathways, and building blocks that support the
              goal.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1.5"
            >
              Build
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Create something clear, useful, and aligned with the work behind
              it.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article
            class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
          >
            <h3
              class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1.5"
            >
              Evolve
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              Refine, expand, or simplify as the work changes.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8 - Questions that shape the site -->
      <section id="questions" class="mb-16 md:mb-20">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3"
        >
          Questions that shape the site
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3 max-w-3xl">
          You do not need perfect answers before starting. But a few clear
          answers can prevent a lot of unnecessary complexity.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1.5 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          <li>
            What should a first-time visitor understand within thirty seconds?
          </li>
          <li>What action should they take next?</li>
          <li>What proof will help them trust you?</li>
          <li>How will people discover the site?</li>
          <li>How often will the site change?</li>
          <li>Who will maintain it?</li>
          <li>What would success look like ninety days from now?</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- What to do next -->
      <section
        id="next"
        class="pt-8 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
      >
        <div
          class="flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4"
        >
          <a
            href="/guides/planning"
            class="inline-block bg-blue-700 text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold hover:bg-blue-800 transition dark:bg-blue-500 dark:hover:bg-blue-400"
          >
            Open Planning
          </a>
          <a
            href="/guides/web-builders"
            class="inline-block border border-blue-700 text-blue-700 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold hover:bg-blue-50 transition dark:border-blue-300 dark:text-blue-300 dark:hover:bg-blue-900/30"
          >
            Read Platform Overview
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="text-center text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mt-3">
          Imagine the structure. Choose the path.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
